From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Diljit Singh <hey@djdev.me>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 07:40:24 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] media: apple: isp: stop and restart T8140 firmware
 across a deep sleep

On T8140 iBoot loads the ISP firmware, and the driver starts it once and
keeps it resident. A sleep that powers the SoC down takes the
coprocessor with it, so the firmware has to be stopped first, and the
camera stayed dead until the next boot.

The firmware rebuilds its own data from a pristine copy early in each
boot, so it can start again from its memory as iBoot handed it over.
Stop it as macOS does for a cold start, and start it again:

- The stop: the stop key request, opcode 0x1d on the DEBUG channel with
  macOS's 1 s timeout, which gives a word of the firmware's memory and
  the value it writes there once stopped; CISP_CMD_STOP; GPR0 set and
  CISP_CMD_POWER_DOWN posted, answered in GPR0; then a wait for the key
  and for the coprocessor's WFI before its domains go off. macOS carries
  on if the key isn't there or a wait times out, and so does this. On
  the IO channel 0x1d is another command, and neither it nor the STOP
  after it was answered, which cost every sleep 6 s.
- The interrupt: the mailbox's enable and route words zeroed, then the
  usual GPR1 write and a 10 us wait.
- The start: the coprocessor's reset vector, which a power-off can
  clear, is noted at the first start and written back, with bit 0 set,
  where it reads lost; one latched elsewhere refuses the start. After a
  restart CONFIG_GET has to report the channels found at probe, or the
  firmware is stopped again.

For a system sleep the firmware is stopped before it, and starts again
in process context: its handshake in the PM resume path held up the
whole resume. A stream that ran across the sleep restarts from a work
item on system_freezable_wq, which runs once tasks thaw, so a stream no
longer refuses the sleep; without one the firmware starts at the next
stream. A stream turned off before the work runs leaves it nothing to
restart, and remove cancels it. Hibernation still leaves the camera
down, since the image resumes in a boot where that memory isn't the
driver's. Stopped firmware that can start again is off, not dead, so a
capture stop only sends its channel commands to running firmware.

T8140's cold stop sends STOP as a command with its reply, as macOS
does, through its own isp_cmd_stop_cold(); isp_cmd_stop() stays as it
was for the other SoCs, which keep their stop and start.

Signed-off-by: Diljit Singh <hey@djdev.me>
Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
